{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QCvUnvWxJmU6Wh3HwYhwv9","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Example: Browser-driven web app\n2\t\n3\tYou have a dev server that serves HTML to a browser. An agent in a\n4\theadless container can't open a browser window - so \"run the app\" means\n5\tlaunching the dev server, driving a headless Chromium against it, and\n6\tproducing a screenshot that proves the page rendered.\n7\t\n8\tDon't write a browser driver. Use `chromium-cli`.\n9\t\n10\t## Dev server\n11\t\n12\tFind the dev command (`package.json` `scripts.dev`, `Makefile`,\n13\tREADME), start it in the background, and wait for it to actually serve:\n14\t\n15\t```bash\n16\tnpm run dev &   # or yarn dev, pnpm dev, make serve, ./dev.sh\n17\ttimeout 30 bash -c 'until curl -sf http://localhost:3000 >/dev/null; do sleep 1; done'\n18\t```\n19\t\n20\tDon't `sleep 5` - poll the port. Stop by killing the port's listener\n21\t-- `lsof -ti:3000 -sTCP:LISTEN | xargs -r kill` - before relaunching,\n22\tor the next run hits `EADDRINUSE`. (`$!` after `npm run dev &` is only\n23\tthe npm wrapper; npm doesn't forward SIGTERM to the server it spawned,\n24\tso the port kill is what actually frees it.) Avoid `pkill -f` with a\n25\tbroad pattern - it can match the agent's own command line and kill the\n26\tsession.\n27\t\n28\t## Drive\n29\t\n30\t`chromium-cli` is a headless-Chromium REPL. Pipe a script to stdin:\n31\t\n32\t```bash\n33\tchromium-cli --session app <<'EOF'\n34\tnav http://localhost:3000\n35\twait-for text=Dashboard\n36\tscreenshot\n37\tclick button:has-text(\"New item\")\n38\tfill input[name=\"title\"] Smoke test\n39\tpress Enter\n40\twait-for text=Smoke test\n41\tscreenshot\n42\tconsole --errors\n43\tEOF\n44\t```\n45\t\n46\tScreenshots land in `chromium_cli/sessions/app/screenshots/` (latest\n47\tsymlinked as `screenshot.png`). That's the whole loop: `nav` ->\n48\t`wait-for` the element you need -> act (`click` / `fill` / `type` /\n49\t`press`) -> `screenshot` -> `console --errors` to check nothing threw.\n50\tFull command reference: `chromium-cli` skill, or `help` at the prompt.\n51\t\n52\tFor iterative debugging, run it under tmux and `send-keys` one command\n53\tat a time - same commands, same session.\n54\t\n55\t**If `chromium-cli` isn't available:** adapt\n56\t[electron.md](electron.md)'s REPL driver - the structure and commands\n57\ttransfer, but it's `_electron`-specific:\n58\timport `{ chromium }` instead, launch with\n59\t`chromium.launch({ args: ['--no-sandbox'] })`, acquire the page via\n60\t`(await app.newContext()).newPage()` then `goto()` your dev URL, and\n61\tdrop the Electron-only window introspection\n62\t(`.windows()`/`.firstWindow()`/the `windows` command).\n63\t\n64\t## What to put in the skill\n65\t\n66\tThe project-specific bits only. `chromium-cli` handles the mechanics.\n67\t\n68\t- **Dev command + port + stop.** The exact start line, any env vars it\n69\t  needs, and the `kill` to stop it.\n70\t- **Auth.** Whatever gets a logged-in session - a `set-cookie` line, a\n71\t  `fill`/`click` login sequence, or a helper script that does the API\n72\t  dance and emits the cookie.\n73\t- **One representative interaction.** Not the whole app - one path that\n74\t  proves it's running, ending in a screenshot.\n75\t- **App-specific gotchas.** Only the ones you actually hit.\n76\t\n77\t## Gotchas that recur\n78\t\n79\t- **React controlled inputs.** `eval el.value = '...'` doesn't fire\n80\t  React's onChange. Use `fill` / `type` - they go through Playwright's\n81\t  input pipeline.\n82\t- **Websockets / long-poll.** `wait-idle` never settles. `wait-for` the\n83\t  element you actually need.\n84\t- **Slow first paint.** Vite/Next compile routes on demand; the first\n85\t  `nav` can take 10s+. `wait-for` handles it; raw `sleep` doesn't.\n86\t- **`screenshot-element <sel>`** crops to one element - use it when the\n87\t  diff is in a specific component, not the whole page.\n88\t- **Check `console --errors` before declaring success.** A page can\n89\t  render its shell while every data fetch 500s.\n90\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"67061852-27be-45cc-903f-7338bae71ccd","uuid":"af60f5fd-374d-4571-9eec-c3a4a3e0463e","timestamp":"2026-10-15T12:04:54.099Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/tmp/claude-1000/bundled-skills/2.1.278/7baae5019b02c56cdfb0ee50dd002c82/run/examples/playwright.md","content":"# Example: Browser-driven web app\n\nYou have a dev server that serves HTML to a browser. An agent in a\nheadless container can't open a browser window - so \"run the app\" means\nlaunching the dev server, driving a headless Chromium against it, and\nproducing a screenshot that proves the page rendered.\n\nDon't write a browser driver. Use `chromium-cli`.\n\n## Dev server\n\nFind the dev command (`package.json` `scripts.dev`, `Makefile`,\nREADME), start it in the background, and wait for it to actually serve:\n\n```bash\nnpm run dev &   # or yarn dev, pnpm dev, make serve, ./dev.sh\ntimeout 30 bash -c 'until curl -sf http://localhost:3000 >/dev/null; do sleep 1; done'\n```\n\nDon't `sleep 5` - poll the port. Stop by killing the port's listener\n-- `lsof -ti:3000 -sTCP:LISTEN | xargs -r kill` - before relaunching,\nor the next run hits `EADDRINUSE`. (`$!` after `npm run dev &` is only\nthe npm wrapper; npm doesn't forward SIGTERM to the server it spawned,\nso the port kill is what actually frees it.) Avoid `pkill -f` with a\nbroad pattern - it can match the agent's own command line and kill the\nsession.\n\n## Drive\n\n`chromium-cli` is a headless-Chromium REPL. Pipe a script to stdin:\n\n```bash\nchromium-cli --session app <<'EOF'\nnav http://localhost:3000\nwait-for text=Dashboard\nscreenshot\nclick button:has-text(\"New item\")\nfill input[name=\"title\"] Smoke test\npress Enter\nwait-for text=Smoke test\nscreenshot\nconsole --errors\nEOF\n```\n\nScreenshots land in `chromium_cli/sessions/app/screenshots/` (latest\nsymlinked as `screenshot.png`). That's the whole loop: `nav` ->\n`wait-for` the element you need -> act (`click` / `fill` / `type` /\n`press`) -> `screenshot` -> `console --errors` to check nothing threw.\nFull command reference: `chromium-cli` skill, or `help` at the prompt.\n\nFor iterative debugging, run it under tmux and `send-keys` one command\nat a time - same commands, same session.\n\n**If `chromium-cli` isn't available:** adapt\n[electron.md](electron.md)'s REPL driver - the structure and commands\ntransfer, but it's `_electron`-specific:\nimport `{ chromium }` instead, launch with\n`chromium.launch({ args: ['--no-sandbox'] })`, acquire the page via\n`(await app.newContext()).newPage()` then `goto()` your dev URL, and\ndrop the Electron-only window introspection\n(`.windows()`/`.firstWindow()`/the `windows` command).\n\n## What to put in the skill\n\nThe project-specific bits only. `chromium-cli` handles the mechanics.\n\n- **Dev command + port + stop.** The exact start line, any env vars it\n  needs, and the `kill` to stop it.\n- **Auth.** Whatever gets a logged-in session - a `set-cookie` line, a\n  `fill`/`click` login sequence, or a helper script that does the API\n  dance and emits the cookie.\n- **One representative interaction.** Not the whole app - one path that\n  proves it's running, ending in a screenshot.\n- **App-specific gotchas.** Only the ones you actually hit.\n\n## Gotchas that recur\n\n- **React controlled inputs.** `eval el.value = '...'` doesn't fire\n  React's onChange. Use `fill` / `type` - they go through Playwright's\n  input pipeline.\n- **Websockets / long-poll.** `wait-idle` never settles. `wait-for` the\n  element you actually need.\n- **Slow first paint.** Vite/Next compile routes on demand; the first\n  `nav` can take 10s+. `wait-for` handles it; raw `sleep` doesn't.\n- **`screenshot-element <sel>`** crops to one element - use it when the\n  diff is in a specific component, not the whole page.\n- **Check `console --errors` before declaring success.** A page can\n  render its shell while every data fetch 500s.\n","numLines":90,"startLine":1,"totalLines":90}}}
